stently.\ndf['zscore_LOC'] = (df['LOC'] - df['LOC'].mean()) / df['LOC'].std()\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 3: Create binary-derived and categorical features\n# Feature: `high_nX`\n# Feature description: Binary column indicating whether the number of halogen atoms (nX) is greater than its median value.\n# Usefulness: Halogen atom presence might strongly influence molecular properties that impact biodegradability. Classifying them as binary might help downstream models.\ndf['high_nX'] = (df['nX'] > df['nX'].median()).astype(int)\n\n# Feature: `categorical_C_percentile`\n# Feature description: Categorical column derived by binning the percentage of C atoms (C%) into quartiles.\n# Usefulness: Groups chemicals into categories based on their carbon content, potentially highlighting structural dependencies with biodegradability.\ndf['categorical_C_percentile'] = pd.qcut(df['C%'], q=4, labels=['Q1', 'Q2', 'Q3', 'Q4'])\n\n# Feature: `binary_N_presence`\n# Feature description: Binary column indicating whether nitrogen atoms (nN) are present in the chemical instance.\n# Usefulness: Nitrogen is an important factor in chemical reactivity, and its presence/absence might correlate directly to biodegradability.\ndf['binary_N_presence'] = (df['nN'] > 0).astype(int)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Dropping irrelevant or redundant columns (if necessary)\n# Explanation why the column nCRX3 is dropped\n# Reason: nCRX3 has very low variance (mostly zeros) and is likely to provide minimal value to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['nCRX3'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column B04[C-Br] is dropped\n# Reason: This binary column has a very low mean (0.0205), suggesting it mostly contains zeros and does not contribute substantially to distinguishing classes.\ndf.drop(columns=['B04[C-Br]'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column Psi_i_1d is dropped\n# Reason: The column has a zero mean and extremely small standard deviation, indicating near-constant values with negligible contribution to t